{:edb/db-before-t 1742 :edb/db-after-t 1743 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535055 50 #inst "2026-10-08T15:45:49.652Z" 13194139535055 true] [17592186161703 1100 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/assets/494cd45f9c85aaa8703ed4c5a0cac805b69cd91bbef979a2c3ff1ffac1592763.png)" 13194139535055 true] [17592186161703 1100 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535055 false] [17592186161703 1145 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21},$ we obtain $R \\neq R^T.$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/assets/4db4bdfa8aaeac26238404107f195847eaccde9e595a7d1a0678053e2b149d0e.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\"" 13194139535055 true] [17592186161703 1145 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n$A = A^T$\n\nEquivalently,\n\n$$\na_{ij} = a_{ji}\n$$\n\nfor all $i,j$. In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T$.\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T$.\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21}$, we obtain $R \\neq R^T$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\"" 13194139535055 false] [17592186161751 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+A^T$ is always symmetric.\n\n- The difference between two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric." 13194139535055 true] [17592186161751 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+A^T$ is always symmetric.\n2. The difference between two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n3. The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric." 13194139535055 false] [17592186161751 1145 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/assets/f8d153708f7872d0c96414a77c12379d40c6b60a5f08844945fb8a8f8c1809ab.png)\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535055 true] [17592186161751 1145 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n$A = A^T$\n\nEquivalently,\n\n$$\na_{ij} = a_{ji}\n$$\n\nfor all $i,j$. In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A$, we have\n$(A + A^{T})^{T}|= A^{T} + (A^{T})^{T}; = A^{T} + A; = A + A^{T}$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n$AB|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535055 false] [17592186161911 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328758 13194139535055 true] [17592186161911 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328765 13194139535055 true] [17592186161911 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328786 13194139535055 true] [17592186161911 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328793 13194139535055 true] [17592186161959 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328772 13194139535055 true] [17592186161959 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328779 13194139535055 true] [17592186161959 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328800 13194139535055 true] [17592186161959 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328807 13194139535055 true] [17592186162512 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535055 true] [17592186162512 1100 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535055 true] [17592186162512 1100 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, Q = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}, R = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535055 false] [17592186162512 1145 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\"" 13194139535055 true] [17592186162608 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535055 true] [17592186162608 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $AA^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n2. The sum of two $n \\cdot n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n3. The product of two $n \\cdot n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." 13194139535055 false] [17592186162608 1100 "Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." 13194139535055 true] [17592186162608 1145 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328758 1017 #edb/ref 17592186328759 13194139535055 true] [17592186328758 1089 "q-140504" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328758 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535055 true] [17592186328758 1100 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328758 1113 #uuid "d998fa5b-63b3-55ca-a8aa-c2f7bc2f2a1c" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328758 1145 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P,$ $Q,$ and $R.$\"" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328759 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328760 13194139535055 true] [17592186328759 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328761 13194139535055 true] [17592186328759 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328762 13194139535055 true] [17592186328759 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328763 13194139535055 true] [17592186328759 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328764 13194139535055 true] [17592186328759 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535055 true] [17592186328759 1041 "selection" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328759 1104 #edb/ref 17592186328762 13194139535055 true] [17592186328759 1110 #uuid "5528bf15-c00b-5418-b305-184e01d61a8a" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328760 1034 "None of the matrices are symmetric" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328760 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535055 true] [17592186328760 1111 #uuid "eedf2233-cd35-51dd-ba8a-acdfb24fada6" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328761 1034 "$P$ and $R$ only" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328761 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535055 true] [17592186328761 1111 #uuid "b4dbaa75-a5a2-5947-9bbd-cbd505eedb99" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328762 1034 "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328762 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535055 true] [17592186328762 1111 #uuid "1abd424b-ab9d-567a-aa9a-392f2bf8a6ce" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328763 1034 "$Q$ and $R$ only" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328763 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535055 true] [17592186328763 1111 #uuid "e02c8b56-9c90-5ce6-98c1-e9cb055d4d29" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328764 1034 "$P$ and $Q$ only" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328764 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535055 true] [17592186328764 1111 #uuid "a11ef24c-e275-5da0-b9cc-7a5868387040" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328765 1017 #edb/ref 17592186328766 13194139535055 true] [17592186328765 1089 "q-143679" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328765 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535055 true] [17592186328765 1100 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328765 1113 #uuid "14c9b2e9-b22a-597e-94a1-26642435278d" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328765 1145 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the matrices are symmetric.\"" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328766 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328767 13194139535055 true] [17592186328766 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328768 13194139535055 true] [17592186328766 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328769 13194139535055 true] [17592186328766 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328770 13194139535055 true] [17592186328766 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328771 13194139535055 true] [17592186328766 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535055 true] [17592186328766 1041 "selection" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328766 1104 #edb/ref 17592186328767 13194139535055 true] [17592186328766 1110 #uuid "a310c5ec-c97a-55cb-ad91-cc33ccba7dea" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328767 1034 "None of the matrices are symmetric" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328767 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535055 true] [17592186328767 1111 #uuid "c076c3e4-ca30-53e1-9053-0585f557ff4c" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328768 1034 "$P$ and $R$ only" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328768 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535055 true] [17592186328768 1111 #uuid "43ebfd72-668a-55f2-ae3e-c80c0a87e537" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328769 1034 "$R$ only" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328769 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535055 true] [17592186328769 1111 #uuid "2ee6595b-4a0e-553c-8249-72f4b6b609b1" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328770 1034 "$Q$ only" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328770 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535055 true] [17592186328770 1111 #uuid "13755144-f63e-5350-80a1-c1a769e70153" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328771 1034 "$P$ only" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328771 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535055 true] [17592186328771 1111 #uuid "f1eaa499-034f-5dec-b795-eabdd59655b0" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328772 1017 #edb/ref 17592186328773 13194139535055 true] [17592186328772 1089 "q-143685" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328772 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535055 true] [17592186328772 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is a square matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric." 13194139535055 true] [17592186328772 1113 #uuid "4fade6e6-11ef-58fb-9f20-801a35d8d789" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328772 1145 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their sum is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"