$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." 13194139535055 true] [17592186328779 1113 #uuid "8ea3443f-216b-53ab-8156-54dba0b0f421" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328779 1145 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ their product $AB$ is also diagonal, and any diagonal matrix is symmetric.\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, $A+{A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A)^{T} & =(A{A}^{T})^{T}-({A}^{T}A)^{T} \\\\  & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535055 true] [17592186328780 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328781 13194139535055 true] [17592186328780 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328782 13194139535055 true] [17592186328780 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328783 13194139535055 true] [17592186328780 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328784 13194139535055 true] [17592186328780 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328785 13194139535055 true] [17592186328780 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535055 true] [17592186328780 1041 "selection" 